{{also|incisión}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kh₂eyd-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|incision}}, from {{der|en|fro|incision}}, from {{der|en|LL.|incisiō}} from the verb {{m|la|incidō|t=I cut into}} + action noun suffix {{m|la|-iō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈsɪʒən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪʒən}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[cut]], especially one made by a [[scalpel]] or similar medical tool in the context of [[surgery|surgical]] [[operation]]; the [[scar]] resulting from such a cut.
#* {{circa|1595}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Richard II (play)|Richard II}},'' Act I, Scene{{nbsp}}1,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=richard2&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: Wrath-kindled gentlemen, be ruled by me;
#*: Let’s purge this [[choler]] without letting blood:
#*: This we prescribe, though no physician;
#*: Deep malice makes too deep '''incision''';
#* '''1922''', {{w|Sinclair Lewis}}, ''{{w|Babbitt}},'' Chapter{{nbsp}}33,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/1156/1156-h/1156-h.htm]</sup>
#*: Gunch was so humorous that Mrs. Babbitt said he must “stop making her laugh because honestly it was hurting her '''incision'''.”
#* '''1999''', {{w|Ahdaf Soueif}}, ''The Map of Love,'' London: Bloomsbury, 2000, Chapter 28, p.{{nbsp}}470,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=osakAwAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: In the midst of the men a black upright stove sends out its heat. On the glowing holes at the top Ya‘qub Artin has carefully placed some chestnuts, each with a neat '''incision''' in its side.
# The act of cutting into a substance.
#* '''1539''', {{w|Thomas Elyot}} (compiler), ''The Castel of Helthe,'' London, Book{{nbsp}}3, Chapter 6,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A21293.0001.001]</sup>
#*: The parte of Euacuation by lettyng of blud, is '''incision''' or cuttyng of the vayne, wherby the bloud, whiche is cause of syckenes or grefe to the hole body, or any particular part therof, doth most aptly passe.
#* '''1649''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''{{w|Eikonoklastes}},'' London, pp.{{nbsp}}94-95,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/eikonoklastesina00milt]</sup>
#*: Never considering {{...}} that these miseries of the people are still his own handy work, having smitt’n them like a forked Arrow so sore into the Kingdoms side, as not to be drawn out and cur’d without the '''incision''' of more flesh.
#* '''1800''', {{w|William Hayley}}, ''An Essay on Sculpture,'' London: T. Cadell Junior and W. Davies, Epistle 4, p.{{nbsp}}89,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004857122.0001.000]</sup>
#*: Mnesarchus, early as a sculptor known,
#*: From nice '''incision''' of the costly stone,
#* '''1964''', {{w|William Trevor}}, ''The Old Boys,'' Penguin, 2014, Chapter{{nbsp}}21,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=CuK2AwAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: Slowly, as meticulously as if engaged upon a surgical '''incision''', Mr Nox opened his mail.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Separation or solution of viscid matter by medicines.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} {{rfdef|en}}
#*{{quote-journal|en|date=7 March 2021|work=The Guardian|author=David Hytner|title=Manchester United catch City cold as Fernandes and Shaw end winning run|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2021/mar/07/manchester-city-manchester-united-premier-league-match-report|passage=Riyad Mahrez also dragged wide after a De Bruyne '''incision'''.}}

